---
import LickliderPortrait from '../components/LickliderPortrait.astro';
import { PUBLICATIONS } from '../data/publications';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const latest = [...PUBLICATIONS].sort((a, b) => b.sortKey.localeCompare(a.sortKey));
---

<BaseLayout
  description="Licklider builds scientific verification infrastructure for AI research agents, starting with statistics."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">nomue</p>
      <h1>Scientific verification<br />for AI research agents</h1>
      <div class="lede">
        <p>
          Research agents can reason, write code, and use statistical software. nomue gives them a separate place to check the parts that should not depend on the model's own judgment.
        </p>
        <p>
          It checks whether a supported statistical analysis can proceed, whether required information is missing, whether reported numbers are consistent with the registered calculation, and when the agent should clarify or refuse.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta">
        <a href="/nomue/">Explore nomue →</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Protocol →</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Verifier →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sections">
    <section class="band band--single">
      <div class="band-inner">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why</p>
        <div class="band-body">
          <p class="lead">
            A model can be useful without being the final authority on whether its own statistical analysis is scientifically supported.
          </p>
          <p>
            In a research workflow, a small mistake can propagate from analysis into figures, results, and claims. nomue separates verification from generation: the research agent can orchestrate the work and explain the result, while a bounded verification capability handles the supported statistical decision and numerical checks.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--single">
      <div class="band-inner band-inner--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">How It Works</p>
        <div class="flow" aria-label="Research agent to nomue verification flow">
          <div class="flow-step">
            <span>1</span>
            <h2>Research agent</h2>
            <p>Understands the task, gathers context, and decides that statistical verification is needed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div class="flow-step">
            <span>2</span>
            <h2>nomue</h2>
            <p>Checks the supported contract and returns execute, clarify, refuse, or a verified numerical result.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div class="flow-step">
            <span>3</span>
            <h2>Research workflow</h2>
            <p>Uses the bounded result without treating the model's self-assessment as the verification step.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="more"><a href="/nomue/">→ How nomue works</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Current Scope</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p>
          The first public Protocol support target is deliberately narrow: independent two-group continuous data using a two-sided Welch t-test, with explicit declarations, numerical recomputation, and local verification of the supported parts of a Record.
        </p>
        <p>
          A paired-t successor is in public RFC review. Other statistical methods are not implied by the current public support target.
        </p>
        <p class="more">
          <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol/issues/25" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">→ Paired-t public RFC</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--single">
      <div class="band-inner band-inner--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">What We Build</p>
        <div class="layers">
          <article>
            <p class="layer-label">Public trust layer</p>
            <h2>Protocol, Record, verifier</h2>
            <p>
              Public semantics define what a supported Record means. The verifier lets anyone check supported properties locally without depending on Licklider's service.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="layer-label">Agent runtime</p>
            <h2>Scientific verification capabilities</h2>
            <p>
              Agent-callable capabilities apply bounded scientific rules, numerical checks, clarification requirements, and refusal boundaries. The runtime can evolve without turning every product implementation detail into a public standard.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p>
          We treat research as part of product development. Numerical edge cases, implementation disagreements, and methodological questions are investigated before they become verification rules.
        </p>
        <p>
          That work has already produced a public preprint, upstream statistical-software findings, and a growing body of reviewed numerical evidence.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/research/">→ Research</a> · <a href="/engineering/">Engineering →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p class="lead">Does adding nomue actually make a research agent more reliable?</p>
        <p>
          We are building a controlled evaluation that compares the same model with the same baseline statistical tools, with and without nomue. The primary comparison isolates the incremental effect of adding nomue.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have not published a comparative performance result yet. The evaluation is designed to make that question measurable rather than assumed.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/evaluation/">→ Evaluation design and status</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--single">
      <div class="band-inner">
        <p class="eyebrow">Thesis</p>
        <div class="band-body">
          <h2 class="section-title">Engineering Accountability at Machine Scale</h2>
          <p>
            AI has scaled generation. As more intellectual work is produced by machines, approval becomes meaningful only when it remains connected to evidence that can be checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            We start with scientific statistics because the connection between data, analytical decisions, numerical results, and claims can be made explicit and independently testable.
          </p>
          <p class="more"><a href="/thesis/">→ Read the thesis</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--single">
      <div class="band-inner band-inner--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why "Licklider"</p>
        <div class="why">
          <div class="band-body">
            <p>
              In "Man-Computer Symbiosis" (1960), J. C. R. Licklider described a division of labor in which humans set goals and exercise judgment while computers carry out routine work.
            </p>
            <p>
              As machines take on more of the generation and execution, we are building infrastructure for the part that must remain accountable: the connection between decisions and evidence.
            </p>
          </div>
          <LickliderPortrait />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="latest" class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Latest</p>
      <div class="band-body latest-body">
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <tbody>
              {
                latest.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="latest-date">{row.date}</td>
                    <td>
                      <a href={row.href}>{row.title}</a>
                    </td>
                    <td class="latest-category">
                      <a href={`/${row.category.toLowerCase()}/`}>{row.category}</a>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint);
  }

  .hero-inner {
    max-width: var(--shell);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 7rem var(--gutter) 6rem;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    max-width: 58rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 4.25rem);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 42rem;
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    color: var(--ink-1);
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 2rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .cta a {
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
    padding-bottom: 3px;
  }

  .sections {
    max-width: var(--shell);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
  }

  .band {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 12rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 3rem;
    padding: 4rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint);
  }

  .band:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .band > .eyebrow {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
  }

  .band-body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .latest-body {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .band--single {
    display: block;
  }

  .band-inner {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .band-inner--wide {
    max-width: 56rem;
  }

  .band-inner > .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.65rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .flow {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .flow-step,
  .layers article {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .flow-step span,
  .layer-label {
    display: block;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .flow-step h2,
  .layers h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .flow-step p,
  .layers p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .flow-arrow {
    align-self: center;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .layers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .why {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 16rem);
    gap: 3.5rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .why .band-body {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .latest-date {
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .latest-category {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    text-align: right;
  }

  .latest-category a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  tbody tr:first-child td {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-faint);
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .hero-inner {
      padding: 4rem var(--gutter) 3.5rem;
    }

    .band {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 1.25rem;
      padding: 3rem 0;
    }

    .flow,
    .layers,
    .why {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .flow-arrow {
      justify-self: center;
      transform: rotate(90deg);
    }

    .why {
      gap: 2.5rem;
    }

    .why :global(.portrait) {
      max-width: 15rem;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }
  }
</style>
